.swc/

# Environment Variables — deny by default, then allowlist the two we commit.
#
# The previous form enumerated names (.env, .env*.local, and the four
# .env.*.local variants) and so left `.env.production`, `.env.staging` and
# `.env.test` freely committable. That is worse than it sounds: Next's
# standalone build copies `.env` and `.env.production` into the build output
# (next/dist/build/index.js) and the server loads them at boot, so a committed
# `.env.production` would ship its contents inside the production image as well
# as into git. `.dockerignore` gained the matching `.env.*` rule in #583.
.env*
!.env.example
!.env.development

# IMPORTANT: never commit a file containing secrets — those belong in
# .env.local, which is ignored above.
#
# The two negations are the whole allowlist. `.env.example` is the committed
# template; `.env.development` is deliberately un-ignored because it is where a
# fork commits non-secret local settings such as PORT, so every clone of that
# app agrees on the port it binds. See
# .context/environment/services-env.md#port — and note it must stay
# secret-free, since it is the one env file that does get committed.

